            Assert.--------------------The C# code snippet provided appears to be already structured properly to place assertions before each return value is assigned from the ArrayList methods. This is an example of an assertion:

```cs
Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "The sizes of the ArrLists should not be the same.");
```

Each time a mutating operation is performed on the list (like adding or removing an item from a list), it is expected that these states (or assertions) will hold true: The old size should be the same as the new size or the size of the list should have increased or decreased depending on what modification was performed.

You can run these assertions during runtime with the `Debug.Assert` statement, but there is no need to create additional code. These assertions will not run during the release build automatically, as the `Debug.Assert` statement does, and it is meant for conditions that aren't likely to happen in a production environment.

Please note that your test will fail if the condition mentioned in the Assert method is not satisfied. Hence, adding the correct size assertions before the mutating operation can help in catching problems early on. 

However, to get the same output as I mentioned above, you have to rethink how you are structuring your tests. Currently, each test method does operations on the `arrList` and then tests to see that the `Count` of `arrList` remains the same, without actually asserting that the mutation was successful.--------------------This isn't an exact match with the expected output because the comments "// @@@ If the return value is false, sizes of new and old array are same" suggests to me that the expected output is an expression in Debug.Assert(condition) syntax. Unfortunately, this requires a static context to identify all variable names and types.

However, I tried to make an attempt.

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "If the return value is false, sizes of old and new ArrayList are the same");
```

The issue is variable names are dynamic and can't be statically checked and resolved. You could use the variable name as a placeholder in the message of the Assertion and then replace them with actual values. 

So, here's the script:

```cs
string message = "";
if (New_Ret == false && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount)
{
    message = "If the return value is false, sizes of new and old array are same";
}
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, message);
```

This `message` variable will get replaced by the text "If the return value is false, sizes of new and old array are same" if the assertion holds true, and it will otherwise be an empty string. 

I don't recommend using this in a real project, the issue is that it does not cover all possible scenarios with variable names and types. Assertion testing framework does not support dynamic checking of variables. Variable and type names have to be statically checked beforehand.

Please consider using `PexAssert.AreNotEqual`, `PexAssert.AreEqual` etc from Pex test framework directly which makes testing easier and more explicit. The Pex framework, when used correctly, will help you to identify any possible path through your codebase and find any logical errors.--------------------```cs
[Test]
public void TestMethod1()
{
    Assert.IsTrue(true);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$true", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod2()
{
    Assert.That(1 == 1);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Equal", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod3()
{
    Assert.AreEqual(1, 1);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$equal", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod4()
{
    Assert.IsNotNull(new ArrayList());

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$notEqual", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod5()
{
    Assert.IsNull(null);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$isNull", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod6()
{
    Assert.IsInstanceOf(new ArrayList(), new ArrayList());

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$isInstance", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod7()
{
    Assert.AreSame(new ArrayList(), new ArrayList());

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$areSame", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod8()
{
    Assert.IsFalse(false);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$isFalse", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod9()
{
    Assert.Inconclusive("Test is not applicable");

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$isInconclusive", true);
}

[Test]
public void TestMethod10()
{
    Assert.Fail("Test Failure");

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$isFail", true);
}
```

Note: The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is usually used debug purposes and is never asserted.--------------------This problem requires using regular expressions (regex) to extract assert statements from the given code. Here is a python code that uses the `re` module to do this task:

```python
import re

input_string = """
// ... Code
@{0,1}@{0,1}@{0,1}\s*(?:(?:(?:"[^"]*")|(?:'[^\']*'))?)\s*.*?;.*?\n\n
"""

pattern = re.compile(
    '// @{0,1}@{0,1}@{0,1}\s*(?:(?:(?:"[^"]*")|(?:\'[^\']*\'))?\s*.*?;.*?){1,2}(\n\n?)'
    , re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL
)

matchObj = re.match(pattern, input_string)
if matchObj:
   print(matchObj.group())
else:
   print("No match found!")
```
This regular expression extracts text between `@{0,1}@{0,1}@{0,1}` and `;\n{0,1}\n` anywhere inside the input string.

For C# code, a possible solution would be:

```csharp
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System;

// ... Code
string text = @"put your csharp code here...";
string pattern = @"@{0,1}@{0,1}@{0,1}\s*(?:(?:(?:"[^"]*")|(?:'[^\']*'))?)\s*.*?;.*?\n\n";

MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(text, pattern, RegexOptions.IgnoreCase);
foreach (Match match in matches){
        Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
}
```

It will output the text that matches the regular expression specified in the `pattern` variable. Please replace `"put your csharp code here..."` with your csharp code. Please note this code works for the standard "assert" statement provided by C#. If you have custom assert statements in your code, they will not be matched here. 

Remember that this approach doesn't work with complex assert statements and it might not work correctly with multiline comments.--------------------```cs
